window.docContents[133].resolve({"/Distance/#section-the-girth-of-complete-bipartite-graphs":{"contents":"A bipartite graph is triangle-free, since a 2-colourable graph contains no\n3-clique (Colorable.cliqueFree), so its girth is at least 4. When both sides\nhave at least two vertices the complete bipartite graph contains a 4-cycle, so its\ngirth is exactly 4.\n\nlemma egirth_completeBipartiteGraph (s t : ℕ) : K[s + 2, t + 2].egirth = 4 := by\n  refine le_antisymm ?_ (four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three (Colorable.cliqueFree\n    ((completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring _ _).colorable : K[s + 2, t + 2].Colorable 2)\n    (by norm_num)))\n  let c : K[s + 2, t + 2].Walk (.inl 0) (.inl 0) :=\n    cons (by simp : K[s + 2, t + 2].Adj (.inl 0) (.inr 0)) <|\n    cons (by simp : K[s + 2, t + 2].Adj (.inr 0) (.inl 1)) <|\n    cons (by simp : K[s + 2, t + 2].Adj (.inl 1) (.inr 1)) <|\n    cons (by simp : K[s + 2, t + 2].Adj (.inr 1) (.inl 0)) nil\n  have hc : c.IsCycle := by simp [c, cons_isCycle_iff]\n  simpa [c] using hc.egirth_le_length\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Girth","header":"11.3.2. The Girth of Complete Bipartite Graphs","id":"/Distance/#section-the-girth-of-complete-bipartite-graphs"},"/Maps/#section-graph-embeddings":{"contents":"section embed\nvariable {V W X : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} {K : SimpleGraph X}\nvariable (f : G ↪g H) (g : H ↪g K)\n\n\nA SimpleGraph.Embedding G H, written G ↪g H, is an embedding V ↪ W (i.e. an\ninjective map) such that ∀ u v, G.Adj u v ↔ H.Adj (f u) (f v). In other words, f\nis an injective homomorphism that also reflects edges:\nH.Adj (f u) (f v) → G.Adj u v. This means that G is isomorphic to an induced\nsubgraph of H.\n\nexample {u v : V} : H.Adj (f u) (f v) ↔ G.Adj u v := f.map_adj_iff\n\n\nIn terms of comap: an injective function f underlies an embedding G ↪g H iff\npulling back H along f gives exactly G. Conversely, comap along any injective\nfunction is an embedding (Embedding.comap), and a special case of this is the\ninclusion of an induced subgraph (Embedding.induce).\n\nexample : H.comap f = G := f.comap_eq\n#check Embedding.comap  -- (f : V ↪ W) (G : SimpleGraph W) : G.comap f ↪g G\n#check Embedding.induce -- (s : Set V) : G.induce s ↪g G\n\n\nGraph embeddings compose:\n\n#check g.comp f -- G ↪g K\n\n\nEvery graph embedding gives rise to a homomorphism and an underlying embedding of\nvertex types.\n\n#check Embedding.toHom  -- .. (f : G ↪g G') : G →g G'\n#check f.toEmbedding    -- V ↪ W\n\n\nend embed\n\n\nRecall pathNat₁ the path graph on ℕ. We can embed this in itself by sending i\nto i + j, for any fixed j : ℕ.\n\n#source pathNat₁\n\n\n/-- The graph embedding pathNat₁ ↪g pathNat₁, with i ↦ i + j. -/\n@[simps]\ndef pathNat₁Translate (j : ℕ) : pathNat₁ ↪g pathNat₁ where\n  toFun i := i + j\n  inj' := add_left_injective j\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    intro i k\n    grind [Function.Embedding.coeFn_mk]\n\n\nWe can embed G into any blow-up G.blowUpGraph W so long as the parts of the\nblow-up are nonempty (the argument f below picks an element of each part).\n\n/-- Embedding of G into G.blowUpGraph W with nonempty parts. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Embedding.blowUp {ι : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph ι) (W : ι → Type*)\n    (f : (i : ι) → W i) : G ↪g (G.blowUpGraph W) where\n  toFun i := ⟨i, f i⟩\n  inj' _ _ h := congrArg Sigma.fst h\n  map_rel_iff' := Iff.rfl\n\n\nFor example, here is the embedding of C[4] into its blow-up with all parts equal to\nFin 3, sending each vertex i : Fin 4 of C[4] to the vertex ⟨i, 0⟩ of the\nblow-up.\n\n#check (Embedding.blowUp C[4] (fun _ => (Fin 3)) (fun _ => 0))\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Morphisms","header":"5.3.2. Graph Embeddings","id":"/Maps/#section-graph-embeddings"},"/Theorems/#section-edge-density-and-uniform-pairs":{"contents":"The edge density between two finite sets s and t of vertices is the fraction of\npairs in s ×ˢ t that are edges. It is a rational number, so we can compute it.\n\n#check edgeDensity_def -- G.edgeDensity s t = #(G.interedges s t) / (#s * #t)\n\n\n#eval C[6].edgeDensity {0, 2, 4} {1, 3, 5} -- 2 / 3\n\n\nA pair (s, t) is ε-uniform if the edges between them are spread evenly: any\ns' ⊆ s and t' ⊆ t containing at least an ε fraction of s and t have edge\ndensity within ε of the density between s and t. This mimics a random\nbipartite graph.\n\n#source IsUniform\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Regularity and Removal*","header":"13.4.1. Edge Density and Uniform Pairs","id":"/Theorems/#section-edge-density-and-uniform-pairs"}});